    "q-328092": {
      "dom_id": "step-q328092",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14212914/assets/4c895d3f634102ff747342616f572b6a2fa8a57c86215f20b47d2187e6e7f96d.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above, where $\\overset{\\to }{DA}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{AB}=b,$ $\\overset{\\to }{BC}=c,$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DE}=d.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{DC}$ in terms of vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$ To denote the corresponding vectors, use $a$, $b,$ $c,$ and $d.$\n\n$\\overset{\\to }{DC}=$  \n {{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "We want to find a \"path\" from $D$ to $C$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{DC}=\\overset{\\to }{DA}+\\overset{\\to }{AB}+\\overset{\\to }{BC}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14212914/assets/cd94c2f43b5da5252879f8acdcc14fc2f951f9a899903482bd3947ca8ec6921e.png)\n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{DA}=a,\\,\\overset{\\to }{AB}=b,\\,\\overset{\\to }{BC}=c.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14212914/assets/e0f53eb04be491e8b84e2c7d5d252ab2c04bdfe372a93a503788a1ee94f63675.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DC} & =\\overset{\\to }{DA}+\\overset{\\to }{AB}+\\overset{\\to }{BC} \\\\  & =a+b+c.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
